Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

selFromPolicy uses a valid destination prefix's mask width to choose the
selector family and copies all sixteen address bytes for AF_INET6, including
IPv4-mapped IPv6 prefixes. Upstream derives the family from the address bytes
and FromIP unmaps those prefixes, so a transport state's legacy selector
cannot equal its canonical AF_INET6 state-scope record. Ordinary IPv4 and
IPv6 prefixes retain their families, and port masks retain their semantics.

A selector with a source and no destination takes its family from the
source mask width in the same way, so an IPv4-mapped IPv6 source with a
128-bit mask is written as IPv6 with all sixteen bytes, and a source with a
32-bit mask stays IPv4. Upstream takes the family from the destination
alone and writes such a source as AF_INET.

An IPv4 address held in sixteen bytes now follows its mask: with a 32-bit
mask it is written as IPv4, with a 128-bit mask as an IPv6 prefix. Upstream
wrote it as AF_INET with the prefix length counted in 128 bits. For a
length above 32 Linux refuses that selector: for AF_INET,
verify_newpolicy_info and verify_selector_prefixlen (net/xfrm/xfrm_user.c,
present from Linux 4.19) answer EINVAL with "Invalid prefix length in
selector (must be <= 32 for IPv4)", so the old reading was a refused
install. A caller that gave a 128-bit mask of 32 bits or fewer was accepted
before and now gets an IPv6 prefix, which is what its mask declares.

Its tests, in xfrm_selector_family_linux_test.go, compare the library's
serialisation of each selector with a selector written by hand:
TestXfrmSelectorFamilyFromMask for the policy and transport state writers,
TestXfrmSelectorFamilySourceOnly, and TestXfrmSelectorFamilySixteenByteIPv4
for prefix lengths 32, 33, 120 and 128.

No upstream pull request is opened yet for this change.

Its hunks need no earlier patch. Its tests set the port masks of
netlink-xfrm-06-selector-port-masks.patch, so they need that patch to
compile.

Applies unchanged to upstream main (e0e26cd, 2026-11-13), on its own, and its
test passes there unprivileged.

Delete this patch when go.mod requires an upstream release that contains the
change.

diff --git a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go b/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
--- a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
+++ b/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
@@ -128,13 +128,37 @@
 	sel.Family = uint16(nl.FAMILY_V4)
 	if policy.Dst != nil {
 		sel.Family = uint16(nl.GetIPFamily(policy.Dst.IP))
-		sel.Daddr.FromIP(policy.Dst.IP)
-		prefixlenD, _ := policy.Dst.Mask.Size()
+		prefixlenD, bits := policy.Dst.Mask.Size()
+		// The mask declares the family; To4 would erase mapped IPv6 prefixes.
+		switch bits {
+		case 32:
+			sel.Family = uint16(nl.FAMILY_V4)
+		case 128:
+			sel.Family = uint16(nl.FAMILY_V6)
+		}
+		if sel.Family == uint16(nl.FAMILY_V6) {
+			copy(sel.Daddr[:], policy.Dst.IP.To16())
+		} else {
+			sel.Daddr.FromIP(policy.Dst.IP)
+		}
 		sel.PrefixlenD = uint8(prefixlenD)
 	}
 	if policy.Src != nil {
-		sel.Saddr.FromIP(policy.Src.IP)
-		prefixlenS, _ := policy.Src.Mask.Size()
+		prefixlenS, bits := policy.Src.Mask.Size()
+		if policy.Dst == nil {
+			// With no destination, the source mask declares the family.
+			switch bits {
+			case 32:
+				sel.Family = uint16(nl.FAMILY_V4)
+			case 128:
+				sel.Family = uint16(nl.FAMILY_V6)
+			}
+		}
+		if sel.Family == uint16(nl.FAMILY_V6) {
+			copy(sel.Saddr[:], policy.Src.IP.To16())
+		} else {
+			sel.Saddr.FromIP(policy.Src.IP)
+		}
 		sel.PrefixlenS = uint8(prefixlenS)
 	}
 	sel.Proto = uint8(policy.Proto)
